1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,550
Forms that work.

2
00:00:04,350 --> 00:00:09,404
The site has a menu, a journal and a team, and no way to get in touch.

3
00:00:09,404 --> 00:00:11,426
Today we add a contact form.

4
00:00:11,426 --> 00:00:16,480
And a form that actually works turns out to mean four separate things,

5
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:19,224
three of which the defaults get wrong.

6
00:00:20,024 --> 00:00:20,862
Two models.

7
00:00:20,862 --> 00:00:26,956
A form field, tied to the page with a parental key, and the contact page itself,

8
00:00:26,956 --> 00:00:29,394
which is an abstract email form.

9
00:00:29,394 --> 00:00:33,659
The editor builds the form in the admin, field by field.

10
00:00:33,659 --> 00:00:36,935
Every submission is stored in the database,

11
00:00:36,935 --> 00:00:40,058
and emailed if the page has a to address.

12
00:00:40,858 --> 00:00:46,149
The template loops over the form and prints each field with its errors.

13
00:00:46,149 --> 00:00:51,514
Leave out the CSRF token and every single submission is a 403 forbidden.

14
00:00:51,514 --> 00:00:55,612
That is the first of the four things, and the easy one.

15
00:00:56,412 --> 00:00:57,564
Second thing.

16
00:00:57,564 --> 00:01:02,617
Submit the form before you have written a thank you page.

17
00:01:02,617 --> 00:01:03,770
Five hundred.

18
00:01:03,770 --> 00:01:05,897
Template does not exist.

19
00:01:05,897 --> 00:01:06,606
And yet.

20
00:01:06,606 --> 00:01:10,950
The submission was saved, and the email was sent.

21
00:01:11,750 --> 00:01:14,396
Think about what the visitor sees.

22
00:01:14,396 --> 00:01:15,563
A server error.

23
00:01:15,563 --> 00:01:19,921
So they assume nothing happened, and they send it again,

24
00:01:19,921 --> 00:01:22,567
and the studio gets two enquiries.

25
00:01:22,567 --> 00:01:27,626
The thank you template is the page template's name plus _landing,

26
00:01:27,626 --> 00:01:29,105
in the same folder.

27
00:01:29,105 --> 00:01:30,272
Write it first.

28
00:01:31,072 --> 00:01:32,023
Third thing.

29
00:01:32,023 --> 00:01:34,164
Refresh the thank you page.

30
00:01:34,164 --> 00:01:38,446
Wagtail renders it as the direct response to the post,

31
00:01:38,446 --> 00:01:42,569
so refreshing makes the browser send the post again.

32
00:01:42,569 --> 00:01:44,789
Two submissions, two emails.

33
00:01:44,789 --> 00:01:46,930
Every refresh, another one.

34
00:01:47,730 --> 00:01:50,785
The fix is the oldest pattern on the web.

35
00:01:50,785 --> 00:01:52,276
Post, redirect, get.

36
00:01:52,276 --> 00:01:57,864
After a good submission, redirect to the contact page with sent equals one,

37
00:01:57,864 --> 00:02:01,069
and render the thank you template for that.

38
00:02:01,069 --> 00:02:06,583
Now the post answers with a 302, and I refreshed the thank you page twice.

39
00:02:06,583 --> 00:02:08,148
Still one submission.

40
00:02:08,948 --> 00:02:09,895
Fourth thing.

41
00:02:09,895 --> 00:02:12,225
The budget question is optional.

42
00:02:12,225 --> 00:02:17,542
But Wagtail builds a dropdown from the editor's choices and nothing else,

43
00:02:17,542 --> 00:02:19,581
so there is no blank option.

44
00:02:19,581 --> 00:02:24,606
Under five thousand is selected by default, and the browser sends it.

45
00:02:24,606 --> 00:02:29,486
Every single enquiry records a budget that the person never picked.

46
00:02:30,286 --> 00:02:33,117
A small custom form builder fixes it.

47
00:02:33,117 --> 00:02:38,931
If a dropdown is optional, put a blank choice in front of the editor's list.

48
00:02:38,931 --> 00:02:44,440
Tell the page to use it, and a budget left blank is now stored as blank.

49
00:02:45,240 --> 00:02:45,931
And spam.

50
00:02:45,931 --> 00:02:50,922
A honeypot is an ordinary text input, pushed off screen with CSS.

51
00:02:50,922 --> 00:02:55,529
People never see it, and keyboard users can not tab into it.

52
00:02:55,529 --> 00:02:58,216
Bots fill in every input they find.

53
00:02:58,216 --> 00:03:03,822
If it comes back filled in, the bot gets exactly the same thank you page,

54
00:03:03,822 --> 00:03:06,125
and nothing is stored or sent.

55
00:03:06,125 --> 00:03:10,348
Do not make it a hidden input, because bots skip those.

56
00:03:10,348 --> 00:03:13,266
And never tell the bot that it failed.

57
00:03:14,066 --> 00:03:17,411
In development, the email does not go anywhere.

58
00:03:17,411 --> 00:03:21,467
The development settings already use the console backend,

59
00:03:21,467 --> 00:03:24,883
so it prints in the terminal running the server.

60
00:03:24,883 --> 00:03:26,591
That is the whole email.

61
00:03:26,591 --> 00:03:28,940
Real sending is episode thirteen.

62
00:03:29,740 --> 00:03:31,280
And here is the form.

63
00:03:31,280 --> 00:03:34,726
Notice contact appeared in the menu on its own,

64
00:03:34,726 --> 00:03:38,758
because of the show in menus default from last episode.

65
00:03:39,558 --> 00:03:43,215
And the thank you page, at its own address.

66
00:03:43,215 --> 00:03:45,936
Refresh it as often as you like.

67
00:03:46,736 --> 00:03:49,250
Commit, and tag.

68
00:03:50,050 --> 00:03:51,926
Next episode, search.

69
00:03:51,926 --> 00:03:57,820
The search app we unboxed in episode two, finally doing something.

70
00:03:58,620 --> 00:04:03,332
Next episode, search.

71
00:04:03,332 --> 00:04:07,820
Thanks for watching.
